In this lesson we'll look at how to solve cubic and quartic equations once they're already written as a product of factors. The big idea is called the null factor law, and it's really simple. If a bunch of things multiplied together equals zero, then at least one of them has to be zero. That's it. So if your polynomial is written as a product, say x minus one times x minus three times x plus two, all you do is set each bracket to zero, one at a time. So x minus one equals zero gives x equals one, x minus three gives x equals three, and x plus two gives x equals negative two. Three brackets, three answers.

What about when a factor shows up more than once? Say you have x minus two, squared, times x plus one. Setting each one to zero still works, except that x minus two gives you the root x equals two, and because it's squared, that root repeats. So your solutions are x equals two, mentioned twice, and x equals negative one. On a graph, that repeated root is the spot where the curve touches the x-axis and bounces back instead of crossing through.

So really, a root, a linear factor, and an x-intercept are three ways of looking at the same thing. Now let's see it in action.